**Grandma** | Townsfolk Power | Limited
__Basics__
Each night, the Grandma may choose exactly two other players. The next day, these selected players have no vote: the Grandma will have two votes instead.
__Details__
Removing players' votes is an immediate ability. Players with removed votes may still vote on public polls, and their vote will appear normally in visible vote counts, but their vote will not count.
The Grandma's vote appears to be a normal vote in visible vote counts, but will count as 0 or 2 votes, depending on whether the Grandma chooses to use their power. The Grandma must select either exactly two players simultaneously, or no players at all.
At the end of the day, the votes are restored (even if the Grandma is dead at that point).
If the Grandma selects a player with several votes (e.g. another Grandma), the targeted player only loses 1 of their votes.
Players with negative votes or no vote will experience no change in vote value (and the Grandma is informed). Special extra votes, such as the Mayor's vote, can never be removed by the Grandma.
The Grandma is not affected by redirections.

__Simplified__
Each night, the Grandma may choose exactly two players. The next day, these selected players' votes won't count: the Grandma will have two votes instead.

__Formalized__
Unique Role
Starting: Manipulate @Self's `hidden public voting power` to `0`
Immediate Night: [Condition: not (@Selection is @Self), Condition: not (@SecondarySelection is @Self)]
  • Manipulate @Self's `hidden public voting power` to `2` (~NextDay)
  • Evaluate:
    ‣ @Selection->PublicVotingPower > 0: Manipulate @Selection's `hidden public voting power` by `-1` (~NextDay)
    ‣ Otherwise: Learn `@Selection could not be manipulated`
  • Evaluate:
    ‣ @SecondarySelection->PublicVotingPower > 0: Manipulate @SecondarySelection's `hidden public voting power` by `-1` (~NextDay)
    ‣ Otherwise:  Learn `@SecondarySelection could not be manipulated`

__Card__
The Grandma knows what's best for town, and so she fills out more than just her own ballot.
